[
    {
        "content": "<p>On the way to integration by parts for Stieltjes vector measures in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39113\">#39113</a>, I have proved Fubini for the integral with respect to vector measures. In doing so, I have encountered a nasty pain point with our current definition of the vector measure integral, so I would like to refactor it, in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40603\">#40603</a>. The new definition is apparently slightly less general, but more practical to use -- and in fact the full generality of the previous definition is still available if needed, although I expect it will never be needed.  <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"382902\">@Yoh Tanimoto</span>, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"613177\">@Oliver Butterley</span>, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"669043\">@Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span>, do you have opinions on the proposed refactor?</p>",
        "id": 603283077,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781514406
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For the record, here is the PR description, containing a few more details:</p>\n<p>Given a bilinear form <code>B</code> on <code>E x F</code> to <code>G</code>, a function <code>f</code> with values in <code>E</code> and a vector measure <code>v</code> with values in <code>F</code>, the integral wrt the vector measure is defined when the function is integrable wrt the measure <code>(v.transpose B).variation</code>, which is the minimal condition for the integral to make sense.</p>\n<p>I have played a lot recently with integrals for vector measures, and I have realized that assuming this minimal condition creates a lot of complications, for essentially no gain. In this PR, I require the stronger condition that the function is integrable wrt <code>v.variation</code>. So, the integrability condition does not depend on <code>B</code> any more. This makes for smoother statements and smoother proofs (especially in the forthcoming Fubini theorem). In all standard applications, <code>B</code> is an isometry, so <code>(v.transpose B).variation = v.variation</code>, and the theory is unchanged.</p>\n<p>Note that the new approach does not lose any generality: in the unlikely event one wants to integrate a function which is only integrable wrt <code>(v.transpose B).variation</code> for some exotic <code>B</code>, then one can integrate with the vector measure <code>v.transpose B</code> (which takes values in <code>E -&gt; G</code>) and the bilinear form which is the function application, i.e., <code>E -&gt; (E -&gt; G) -&gt; G</code>.</p>",
        "id": 603283278,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781514447
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And here is the forthcoming version of Fubini, where you can imagine why having the definition of integration only depend on the measure can be handy:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">integral_integral_swap</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsFiniteMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ν</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">variation</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsFiniteMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">variation</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">⦄</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CompleteSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CompleteSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Integrable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">uncurry</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">variation</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ν</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">variation</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∫ᵛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∫ᵛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∂</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ν</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∂</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∫ᵛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∫ᵛ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∂</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∂</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">D</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ν</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 603283943,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781514586
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In the original PR (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28499\">#28499</a>) by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"382902\">@Yoh Tanimoto</span> there was actually no <code>transpose</code> before <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28499/changes/f8a9dc7a32cbf11f114876fd28b627d8be730541\">this commit</a>.</p>",
        "id": 603289196,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1781515614
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>NB: the other currently open PRs on the way to the main result are:</p>",
        "id": 603325934,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781522627
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40384\">#40384</a> (+303/-3): the semivariation of a vector measure</p>",
        "id": 603326070,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781522657
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40602\">#40602</a> (+87/-57): move strong measurability proof to <code>setToFun</code></p>",
        "id": 603326212,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781522690
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>From all I remember of the design decisions, that looks a good refactor to me! Great to see <code>integral_integral_swap</code> coming soon!</p>",
        "id": 603328231,
        "sender_full_name": "Oliver Butterley",
        "timestamp": 1781523125
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I was not involved in any of these discussions, but this seems perfectly reasonable to me.</p>",
        "id": 603345153,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1781526425
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm in favor of this change. I believe integrating w.r.t. <code>v.variation</code> is certainly enough for both the formalization of the spectal theorem and the one of stochastic integral</p>",
        "id": 603346757,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1781526737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>sorry that my last change made trouble. I thought more integrable functions were better for some possibly degenerate <code>B</code>. In fact, for concrete applications I have in mind, <code>B</code> can be seen as an isometry (either the map from a Hilbert space into its dual, or the space of bounded operators into itself).</p>\n<p>As such (remotely possible) cases can be in principle treated, I agree that the refactor is indeed better, thanks <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110050\">@Sébastien Gouëzel</span> .</p>",
        "id": 603429274,
        "sender_full_name": "Yoh Tanimoto",
        "timestamp": 1781541212
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No need to be sorry, it's just by experimenting with the notion that we can converge to the most robust design. And this experimention is only possible thanks to your implementation!</p>",
        "id": 603437654,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1781542993
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This seems better to me too.</p>",
        "id": 603469020,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1781549431
    }
]